---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import Faq from "~/components/Faq.astro";
import CtaBand from "~/components/CtaBand.astro";
import { faqNode } from "~/lib/schema";
import { site } from "~/data/site";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why weekly releases instead of daily?",
    a: "Because a page needs to sit still to be measured. Search Console finalises data about three days after the fact and ranking changes take weeks to settle, so a system editing pages daily would be reacting to noise and could never attribute a result to a cause. Daily observation feeds a weekly release, and each changed page is then frozen for its measurement window.",
  },
  {
    q: "What data does Autopilot read?",
    a: "Google Search Console for queries, impressions, clicks and position; GA4 for behaviour and conversion events; a full crawl of the site for structural health; Core Web Vitals field data for real-user performance; and server logs for which AI crawlers fetched which pages. It reads more than it writes, by a wide margin.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do you need access to our hosting?",
    a: "No. Autopilot works through your git repository and your deployment pipeline. It opens pull requests and triggers builds the same way a developer on your team would, which means your existing review, permissions and audit trail all still apply.",
  },
  {
    q: "What if we want to stop it?",
    a: "One command halts all autonomous activity immediately, and it does not require the person who set it up to be available. Everything Autopilot has built stays yours: the site, the repository, the history and the changelog. There is no lock-in mechanism because there is nothing proprietary in the output.",
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="How Autopilot works — measure, decide, ship, prove"
  description="Autopilot observes daily, releases weekly, and freezes each changed page for a fixed measurement window so results can be attributed. Here is the full cycle."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "How it works", href: "/how-it-works/" }]}
  schema={[faqNode(faqs)]}
>
  <section class="section head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">The cycle</p>
      <h1>Slow enough to be measurable. Fast enough to compound.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The hard part of automating website optimisation is not making changes. It is knowing
        which change caused which result — and that requires deliberately going slower than the
        technology allows.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap phases">
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p class="phase-n mono">Phase 01 · every day</p>
        <h2>Measure</h2>
        <p class="lede">Read everything, change nothing.</p>
        <div class="cols">
          <div>
            <h3>Search</h3>
            <p class="muted">Queries, impressions, clicks and average position from Search Console, restricted to final-state data so numbers cannot shift under the analysis.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Behaviour</h3>
            <p class="muted">Sessions, engagement and conversion events from GA4, joined to the landing page so search demand and on-page outcome sit in one table.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Structure</h3>
            <p class="muted">A full crawl: status codes, redirect chains, canonical conflicts, orphaned pages, duplicate titles, invalid structured data.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Speed</h3>
            <p class="muted">Core Web Vitals field data at the 75th percentile, against Google's published thresholds of 2.5s LCP, 200ms INP and 0.1 CLS.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>AI visibility</h3>
            <p class="muted">Which answer-engine crawlers fetched which URLs, and referral traffic arriving from AI assistants — the closest available proxy for citation.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Competitors</h3>
            <p class="muted">Where rivals are answering questions you are shown for but do not serve, so gaps surface as demand rather than as opinion.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p class="phase-n mono">Phase 02 · weekly</p>
        <h2>Decide</h2>
        <p class="lede">Rank by expected value, not by ease.</p>
        <p>
          Every candidate change is scored on the size of the demand behind it, the distance
          between current and achievable performance, and the risk if it is wrong. The output is
          an ordered queue, and only the top of that queue is allowed to ship in a given week.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each candidate carries a written hypothesis before it is permitted to proceed. If a
          change cannot be expressed as "we expect X to move because Y", it does not ship —
          not because the rule is elegant, but because an unfalsifiable change can never be
          judged afterwards.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p class="phase-n mono">Phase 03 · weekly</p>
        <h2>Ship</h2>
        <p class="lede">One change, one commit, one immutable build.</p>
        <p>
          Changes in the safe class are committed and deployed unattended. Everything else opens
          a pull request describing the hypothesis, the diff and the expected effect, and waits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Content is stored as structured data with a validated schema, so the system edits
          fields rather than rewriting page code. An edit that violates the schema — a title too
          long for a search result, a missing description, a malformed date — fails the build and
          never reaches production.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p class="phase-n mono">Phase 04 · 28 days</p>
        <h2>Prove</h2>
        <p class="lede">Compare against pages that were left alone.</p>
        <p>
          A changed page is frozen for its measurement window. At the end, its movement is
          compared with the control group — comparable pages that were deliberately excluded from
          optimisation and therefore absorbed the same algorithm updates and seasonal swings.
        </p>
        <p>
          Changes that beat the control are kept and the pattern is reused. Changes that lose are
          reverted, and the revert is published in the changelog with the same prominence as
          anything that worked.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section guard invert">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">Guard rails</p>
      <h2>What stops it running away</h2>
      <div class="grid grid--2 rails">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>A capped blast radius</h3>
          <p class="muted">A fixed maximum number of pages may change per cycle. Exceeding it requires a human, regardless of how confident the system is.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>An automatic circuit breaker</h3>
          <p class="muted">If overall clicks or conversions fall beyond a set threshold, autonomous releases stop and the last change is rolled back without waiting for anyone.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>A change freeze per page</h3>
          <p class="muted">A page that has just changed cannot change again until its measurement window closes, so results stay attributable to a single cause.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>A kill switch</h3>
          <p class="muted">One command halts everything. It works whether or not the person who configured the system is available.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq faqs={faqs} title="How it works in practice" />
  <CtaBand />
</Base>

<style>
  .head h1 { margin-block: 1rem 1.2rem; max-width: 20ch; }
  .phases ol { list-style: none; padding: 0; counter-reset: phase; }
  .phases > ol > li { padding-block: var(--section); border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .phases h2 { font-size: var(--step-3); margin-block: 0.6rem 0.8rem; }
  .phases > ol > li > p { max-width: 68ch; }
  .phases > ol > li > p + p { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .phase-n { color: var(--accent); font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .cols { display: grid; gap: 1.5rem 2rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  @media (min-width: 700px) { .cols { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }
  .cols h3 { font-size: var(--step-0); margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .cols p { font-size: var(--step--1); }
  .guard h2 { margin-block: 0.8rem 2rem; }
  .rails h3 { font-size: var(--step-1); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .rails p { font-size: var(--step--1); }
</style>
